{#
  Custom error page example (10.6).

  In production (APP_DEBUG off) the framework renders HTML errors through
  host templates when they exist, looked up in this order:

      views/errors/{status}.twig      e.g. errors/404.twig, errors/503.twig
      views/errors/{4xx|5xx}.twig     status-class fallback
      built-in minimal page           when neither exists (or a template throws)

  Available context:
      status        the HTTP status code (int)
      message       the client-safe message (already production-filtered)
      request_path  the path that errored

  A template error never breaks the response — it is logged to
  var/logs/view.log and the built-in page is served instead. In debug mode
  the rich debug page always wins; API/JSON responses are never templated.
#}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>{{ status }} — {{ message }}</title>
    <style>
        :root { color-scheme: light dark; }
        body {
            margin: 0; min-height: 100vh;
            display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
            font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", sans-serif;
            background: #101218; color: #d8dce3;
        }
        main { text-align: center; padding: 2rem; }
        .status { font-size: 5rem; font-weight: 800; margin: 0; color: #818cf8; }
        p { color: #9aa4b2; }
        a { color: #818cf8; text-decoration: none; }
        a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
<main>
    <p class="status">{{ status }}</p>
    <p>{{ message }}</p>
    <p><a href="/">Back to the homepage</a></p>
</main>
</body>
</html>
